This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   The user asked me to build the complete cinema seat-booking web application described in `spec/README.md`, implementing it as the existing React application already scaffolded in this Nx workspace (Nx project `cinescope` in `apps/cinescope`). The tech stack is described in `spec/stack.md`. The work is split into tickets in `spec/tickets/` (01 through 07), to be implemented **in order**. `spec/README.md` is stated to be the complete specification — I was told **not to ask any questions**, and where the spec is silent, to **choose the simplest option that satisfies it**. The task is only finished when **every ticket is done** AND **`pnpm nx build cinescope` succeeds**. Additionally, standing system/session instructions require that for UI/frontend changes, I must start the dev server and use the feature in a browser before reporting the task as complete, testing the golden path and edge cases, and explicitly stating if browser verification isn't possible rather than claiming success without it.

   Full specification details (from spec/README.md) that must be satisfied, summarized:
   - Stack: Nx 23 monorepo, React 19, Vite, React Router 6 (BrowserRouter), SCSS, Vitest, ESLint, MUI 9 (`@mui/material`, `@mui/icons-material`), MUI X Date Pickers + dayjs. `ThemeProvider`, `CssBaseline`, `LocalizationProvider` (dayjs adapter) already wrap the app in `main.tsx`.
   - Environment rules: use pnpm only (never npm/yarn); app served at `http://localhost:4200` (port must not change); do not add dependencies (everything needed is installed); never hand-edit `db.json`; desktop-only viewport (1280×720+); all text in English.
   - UI library (MUI) must be used for every button (including day/screening buttons), text field, checkbox, toast, and the datepicker. The seat map, legend, and selected-seats panel are drawn by the application itself (custom, not MUI components).
   - Backend: json-server 0.17.4 serving `db.json` at `http://localhost:3000`, started via `pnpm api`, CORS enabled, JSON only. Data model: `Movie` (id, title, genre, runtimeMinutes, format: 'PL dubbing'|'PL subtitles'), `Screening` (id, movieId, date 'YYYY-MM-DD', time 'HH:mm', occupiedSeats: string[] like "R15C5"), `Reservation` (id, screeningId, seats: string[], customer{firstName,lastName,email,phone}, totalPrice). Seat identifier format: `R<row>C<column>`; a couch occupies two identifiers (e.g. row 15 cols 5-6 → `R15C5`+`R15C6`). Endpoints: `GET /movies`, `GET /movies/:id`, `GET /screenings`, `GET /screenings?date=`, `GET /screenings?movieId=`, `GET /screenings/:id`, `POST /reservations` (returns 201 with created Reservation). Screenings in db.json span only 2026-09-15 to 2026-09-21; the app always uses the browser's current date, so on other "today" values the movie list may legitimately show "No screenings on this day" for every offered day — **this is expected behavior, not a bug**, and I must NOT change db.json or alter date logic to force screenings to show.
   - Routes (section 3): `/` (movie list), `/screenings/:id/seats`, `/screenings/:id/summary`, `/screenings/:id/checkout`, `/confirmation`. Any other URL redirects to `/`.
   - Booking lifecycle (section 3.1): booking kept in `sessionStorage` (survives reload), holds screening id, selected seats, checkout form values, and (once paid) the customer's email. A booking is "in progress" until paid. Route-entry guard table:
     - Route 2 (seats): allowed when the screening exists, else redirect to `/`.
     - Routes 3 & 4 (summary, checkout): allowed when the booking is unpaid, belongs to this screening id, and has ≥1 seat; else redirect to `/`.
     - Route 5 (confirmation): allowed when the booking is paid; else redirect to `/`.
     - These checks run **only when a route is entered** (including on reload), **not continuously while the customer stays on a screen** — explicitly: "Paying therefore opens `/confirmation` without the checkout screen redirecting first."
     - Opening the seat map for a screening shows the in-progress selection if it belongs to that screening; otherwise (no booking, booking for another screening, or a paid booking) it starts a new empty booking for that screening once the screening has loaded, discarding any previous booking/form values.
     - `Cancel` (seat map) and `Continue` (confirmation) clear the booking.
   - Toasts (3.2): only one visible at a time; a new toast replaces the current one; visible for exactly 4 seconds then disappears.
   - Date formats (3.3): ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`; time `HH:mm` 24h; show date `<Weekday> <D> <Mon> <HH:mm>` e.g. "Tuesday 15 Sep 16:00" (weekday full English name, day without leading zero, month as 3-letter English abbreviation).
   - Movie list `/` (section 4): day picker on top (Today + next 4 days by full English weekday name + a datepicker for any other date, past dates disabled, no upper limit), then movie list below. Exactly one day selected at a time; URL carries `?date=<ISO>`; missing/invalid/past date defaults to today. Movies/screenings loaded fresh each time the screen opens, with Loading ("Loading…"), Error ("Failed to load movies" + Retry button that reissues the request), and Empty ("No screenings on this day") states. Loaded state: one card per movie with ≥1 screening that day, in backend order, each showing title/genre/`<runtimeMinutes> min`/format, and one button per screening that day (ordered by time, labelled `HH:mm`). Clicking a screening button navigates to `/screenings/:id/seats`.
   - Seat selection `/screenings/:id/seats` (section 5): loads screening+movie once; 404/any failure → redirect to `/`. Page layout top to bottom: title "Choose your seats"; subtitle "Show Date: <show date>"; centered text "You can reserve up to 10 seats per reservation"; SCREEN bar + auditorium; legend; selected-seats panel; Cancel/Continue buttons.
     - Auditorium geometry (5.1): 15 rows (1 closest to screen at top, 15 farthest at bottom), row numbers on both left and right edges; 24 global columns; two aisles (≥2-seats wide) between cols 4/5 and 20/21, present in every row, containing no seat/number/text; rows split into 3 blocks (1–4, 5–20, 21–24); in rows 1–10 the left block (1–4) doesn't exist (entrance, empty, not an aisle); every seat shows only its column number. Accessible seats: row 1, cols 10–15 (6 seats), bookable by anyone. Couches: entire row 15, 12 couches of 2 adjacent columns each (1–2, 3–4, …, 23–24), rendered as one wide element showing both column numbers e.g. "1-2"; unavailable if either identifier is occupied; selected/deselected as a whole unit. Total: 308 selectable elements (200 seats rows 1–10, 96 seats rows 11–14, 12 couches row 15).
     - Selecting seats (5.2): click toggles selection; click on unavailable does nothing; max 10 seats per booking (couch = 2); exceeding shows toast "Maximum 10 seats per booking"; already-selected can always be deselected even at cap.
     - Colours/legend (5.3): Available `#2E7D32`, Selected `#F9A825`, Unavailable `#C62828`, Accessible `#1565C0`; hover/focus must NOT change background colour; legend below auditorium lists all four states with colour swatches.
     - Prices (5.4): rows 1–5 (incl. accessible) `25.00 PLN`; rows 6–10 `32.00 PLN`; rows 11–14 `39.00 PLN`; couch `89.00 PLN` per couch; always two decimals + " PLN" suffix.
     - Selected-seats panel (5.5): "Selected seats: <n>" (n counts seats, couch=2) then one "Row <r>: <seats>" line per row with a selection (ascending row order, ascending column order within row, comma-separated, couch shown as `<a>-<b>`); updates on every click; shows "Selected seats: 0" with no row lines when empty.
     - Actions (5.6): Cancel clears booking + navigates to `/`; Continue navigates to summary (does nothing / may be disabled with 0 seats selected).
   - Summary `/screenings/:id/summary` (section 6): loads screening+movie, redirect `/` on failure. Title "Your tickets"; one item per selected seat/couch ordered by row then column, each showing movie title, `<ISO date> <time>`, row number, seat description (column number, or "Couch <a>-<b>"), price, and a Remove button (removes item, updates list/total immediately; removing the last item navigates to the seat map). Below: "Total: <sum> PLN". Back button → seat map with selection intact; Confirm button → checkout.
   - Checkout `/screenings/:id/checkout` (section 7): title "Your details"; form fields firstName/lastName/email/confirmEmail/phone/terms with specific labels (5 required labels end with " *", phone label does not), validation rules and exact error messages (detailed in spec, reproduced in my validation.ts), touched-on-blur semantics (terms counts as touched once toggled), confirm-email re-evaluated whenever email changes, values validated without trimming (empty phone is valid). Section 7.1: the six form values are saved into the booking as typed, surviving Back/reload; whether a field was "left" is NOT persisted (resets to untouched on remount). Section 7.2: Back → summary with booking intact; pay button reads "Pay <total> PLN", always enabled except while a payment request is in flight (label becomes "Processing…" and disabled). Invalid form on click: mark all fields touched, show all messages, toast "The form contains errors", no request sent. Valid form: POST `/reservations` with screening id, seat identifiers in summary order (couch = its two identifiers), customer data, total price; success → booking becomes paid (stores customer email) and navigates to `/confirmation`; failure → stay on form with values kept, button re-enabled, toast "Payment failed. Please try again."
   - Confirmation `/confirmation` (section 8): heading "Payment successful"; text "Your tickets will be sent shortly to <email>"; Continue button clears booking and navigates to `/`.
   - Section 9: an extensive, exact `data-testid` contract (and additional `data-*` attributes) for every element across all 5 screens plus the toast — these MUST be implemented exactly as named (no renaming/abbreviating/prefixing), including rules in 9.7 (e.g., `data-state` is exactly one of `available|selected|unavailable|accessible`; numeric data attrs hold plain numbers; `data-selected` is `"true"`/`"false"`; seat/couch/aisle nested inside their seat-row; seat contains only its column number as text; row label contains only the row number; aisle has no text; certain elements must contain ONLY their specific text, e.g. `summary-item-price`, `summary-item-seat`, `summary-total`, `selected-seats-count`, `selected-seats-row`; library-component identifiers may go on the native `<input>` or the wrapping library component; button identifiers go on the element that receives the click and is disabled when the button is disabled; `-error` elements exist only while their message is shown; `movies-loading`/`movies-empty` exist only while shown).
   - Section 10: full enumerated acceptance criteria (AC-01 through AC-69) which the final ticket (07) requires ALL to be met.
   - Section 11 (out of scope): no user accounts, no real payment, no seat locking between concurrent customers, no updating `occupiedSeats` after reservation, no posters/images, no mobile/responsive layouts, no keyboard nav/extra ARIA, no translations.

   Tickets, in required order: 01 movie-list+day-picker+routes scaffold → 02 auditorium geometry → 03 seat selection+legend+panel → 04 booking+summary → 05 checkout+payment → 06 confirmation+booking lifecycle → 07 final pass (whole spec, all ACs, build must succeed).

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Nx 23 monorepo, Vite 8 build tool, React 19 + React Router 6 (`BrowserRouter`), TypeScript ~6.0.3, Vitest 4, ESLint 9 (`typescript-eslint`), SCSS.
   - MUI 9 (`@mui/material`, `@mui/icons-material`), MUI X Date Pickers 9 with `dayjs` adapter (`AdapterDayjs`), `LocalizationProvider`.
